\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 Dear Editor-in-Chief of IEEE Sensors Letters,\par
\par
We are submitting our manuscript titled \b "Breaking the Affective Invariance Assumption: A Distribution-Level Analysis of Physiological and Subjective Divergence"\b0  for consideration as a Regular Letter. Below, we explicitly address the four required points:\par
\par
\b 1) Statement on the manuscript's significance to the sensors community:\b0\par
This manuscript addresses a fundamental flaw in how physiological sensor data (such as EDA and ECG) is utilized for affective state prediction. By analyzing high-resolution data from the widely-used WESAD dataset, we demonstrate that identical physiological sensor distributions can yield entirely divergent subjective meanings depending on the context. This signifies a paradigm shift for the sensor community: demonstrating that single-modality sensors must be integrated with contextual awareness rather than relying on assumed invariant mappings.\par
\par
\b 2) Comments on the manuscript's novelty, impact, and urgency that require rapid dissemination:\b0\par
Current state-of-the-art affective computing models urgently need reform because they rely on ecologically invalid interpolation of block-level subjective labels to continuous physiological data. We mathematically prove that for approximately 27% of subjects, the physiological distribution during a stress task overlaps by over 87% with a baseline resting task. Prompt publication of this Letter is crucial as it disrupts the prevailing "state-determination" paradigm and guides researchers toward a novel "meaning-oriented" sensor framework.\par
\par
\b 3) Your previously published or submitted works on similar topics (maximum of two citations):\b0\par
Our related work on utilizing longitudinal web-based sensing for affective monitoring has been submitted and is currently under review:\par
- K. Furukawa, "Unobtrusive Web-Based Sensing Framework for Longitudinal Affective Monitoring: A Single-Case Experimental Design Approach to the 'Safe Haven' Effect", IEEE Transactions on Affective Computing (Under Review).\par
(Note: The current submission focuses exclusively on the methodological limits of physiological invariance, which is distinct from the longitudinal behavioral study mentioned above. A comparison emphasizing this distinction is discussed in the manuscript.)\par
\par
\b 4) Preprint repositories:\b0\par
In order to avoid possible rejection due to a high similarity percentage, we confirm that this paper has NOT been posted on any preprint repositories.\par
\par
We believe our findings will provoke critical discussion within the IEEE Sensors community. Thank you for your consideration.\par
\par
Sincerely,\par
